#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1c4e02bb-5ec0-572e-92ba-46d16c6f03fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4819" :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 9 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nSince $A\\sim B$, the equation\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nB\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} + \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and\n\n$$\nx_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4\n$$\n\nwhere $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x_{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x_{4} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} = x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}, x_{4} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e66c269-233b-57f8-9dae-46eb78c228bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for a Given Null Space"}] :question/id #uuid "1cbcbca0-c8b5-55c9-af93-9b9c0ba59ffb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4820" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." :question/worked-solution "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} - x_{4} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-x_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c70963f-589d-532b-9286-559f68693df7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix Given in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a9d2ed82-2ba5-53a6-973d-c859ea5c295f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4821" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$.\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; x_{3} - 4x_{4} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4x_{4} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}$. This implies that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd5a675-c692-505e-900b-d7500fb7c653" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix That Is Not in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "ef3be538-d974-5371-bd5b-a7e2ef3df827" :question/math-academy-id "e-4822" :question/problem "Given the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} = 0; x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nThis implies that the only solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf{0} \\}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is the empty set, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\,\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cba14b5-62f7-5fbe-beb1-9b1511305cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Null Space of a Matrix Given a Row Equivalent Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f21724-0d29-59b2-ac73-88873f3bcb4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118132} {:answer/id #uuid "a25e6ddc-91d2-5af6-ae7f-f8dd8c651fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118133} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb7102d-6897-5c85-924d-e064f376f34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118134} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7f5681-6edb-5e79-af45-d3326f2569e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118135} {:answer/id #uuid "77c46e7f-02a8-5ea9-8291-58b645114626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77c46e7f-02a8-5ea9-8291-58b645114626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118136} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d821182-1acf-5fba-ae3f-2b2f578c8698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118131}] :question/id #uuid "7788836f-aadb-5c09-b706-0eb988c022c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-34522" :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $Null(A)$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c70963f-589d-532b-9286-559f68693df7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis for the Null Space of a Matrix Given in Reduced Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb19d5ad-d1d4-5edc-8758-c8051d6a335f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118360} {:answer/id #uuid "30e4e4d7-c958-52d4-a6ff-b19edf166770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118361} {:answer/id #uuid "65927805-03e2-573b-ab4a-358be0557d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118362} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6366f9-0eb8-59b5-8d49-fbce7f4fa000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118363} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3757d1-19c7-56dc-9efd-b46de9f23ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6366f9-0eb8-59b5-8d49-fbce7f4fa